From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 02:08:44 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: um: implement SME task state for Darwin guests

Bridge ABI 16 lets the Darwin host implement SME for a run. When it
does, darwin_uml_guest_sme_vector_length() reports the host's streaming
vector length (SVL), and the kernel now gives each task what
arch/arm64/kernel/fpsimd.c gives it on a CPU whose only SME vector
length is that SVL. When it reports zero, every new path is inactive
and the kernel behaves as before.

Every arm64 bridge tail grows from 528 to 552 bytes: the FP registers
are followed by TPIDR2_EL0, a pointer to the task's SME storage and its
length. The storage holds Z0-Z31, P0-P15, ZA and ZT0 and is allocated
the first time the host reports an SME access (event 11) or the kernel
needs it. PSTATE.SM and PSTATE.ZA live in the task's SVCR, which the
host no longer forces to zero.

The kernel duties follow native arm64:

- A syscall from streaming mode leaves it first: SM and the vector
  state are cleared and FPSR reads as the native SMSTOP leaves it,
  while ZA is kept.
- Signal frames carry sve_context with SVE_SIG_FLAG_SM, tpidr2_context,
  za_context and, with SME2 and ZA on, zt_context, moving the tail
  into extra_context when it does not fit. A handler starts with SM
  and ZA off and TPIDR2 zero; sigreturn parses the frame as
  parse_user_sigframe() does and rejects what native arm64 rejects.
- ptrace gains NT_ARM_TLS, NT_ARM_SSVE, NT_ARM_ZA and NT_ARM_ZT in a
  regset view that exists only with SME.
- AT_MINSIGSTKSZ reports the largest frame, PR_SME_SET_VL and
  PR_SME_GET_VL and abi.sme_default_vector_length accept and report
  the one SVL.
- clone() with CLONE_VM gives the child no ZA and a zero TPIDR2; a
  fork copies both. exec and task release free the storage.

asm/elf.h now includes the native asm/hwcap.h, which uapi/asm/ptrace.h
also pulls in, and replaces its ELF_HWCAP, ELF_HWCAP2 and ELF_HWCAP3, so
every file sees the Darwin guest's hwcaps and binfmt_elf still emits no
AT_HWCAP3.

The ABI 16 copies also rename the unused state_flags word of the guest
registers to dispatch_flags and define its first flag, which nothing
sets yet.

The x86_64-userspace slice never enables the code.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
